WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.601
-A movie star right there.
Thanks so much for being here.

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:04.868
-Yeah, I felt a little
challenged

00:00:04.868 --> 00:00:06.167
a little bit, like Chevalier,

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:07.367
And I said, "Let me spruce up my
outfit a little bit."

00:00:07.367 --> 00:00:08.601
-Yeah, you really did.

00:00:08.601 --> 00:00:11.200
By the way, you crushed it
in this movie here.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.868
Look at you in "Chevalier."

00:00:13.868 --> 00:00:15.100
Yeah. Yeah.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.834
Now, you actually are
a musician in real life.

00:00:17.834 --> 00:00:18.968
-Yes.
-You are.

00:00:18.968 --> 00:00:20.400
-I'm from New Orleans.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.968
You know, everybody seems like
they're from -- a musician.

00:00:21.968 --> 00:00:23.300
You from New Orleans?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:24.501
-Some New Orleans out there.
-New Orleans shout-out!

00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:26.734
-Yeah. Everyone in
New Orleans is a musician?

00:00:26.734 --> 00:00:27.767
-Well, pretty much.

00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:28.968
-I thought this
was kind of cool.

00:00:28.968 --> 00:00:31.501
This is you in the movie,
and this is you as a kid.

00:00:31.501 --> 00:00:33.300
-With that bald head.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.567
-Yeah. Come on.
You did it, right?

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:36.467
You did it, bud.

00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:44.567
And I was lucky enough to meet
your beautiful mom backstage.

00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:46.501
And she's a singer.

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:49.000
-She's a singer,
and my dad's a musician.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.367
He's a saxophonist.

00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:53.267
We used to -- You know, we used
to play in the church,

00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:54.501
so it was -- We call it --

00:00:54.501 --> 00:00:57.100
We have a group chat
called The Jackson 5.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.701
But we're really
the Harrison family.

00:00:58.701 --> 00:00:59.868
You know what I mean?

00:00:59.868 --> 00:01:02.133
So, it's five of us.
It's my twin sisters.

00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:03.901
They play the flute.
I play the keyboard.

00:01:03.901 --> 00:01:06.133
My mom sings.
My dad plays the sax.

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:08.067
Yeah, yeah, it was a little --

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.534
It's a traveling little
Christian band.

00:01:10.534 --> 00:01:15.634
-Was the plan always to be
an actor or be a musician?

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:17.801
-I didn't know I wanted to be
an actor, per se,

00:01:17.801 --> 00:01:19.934
but I did know -- I kind of
wanted to be a musician.

00:01:19.934 --> 00:01:24.667
Well, I wanted -- Okay,
so, I never told anybody this,

00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:26.534
but I did want to be a musician,

00:01:26.534 --> 00:01:28.968
but I specifically
wanted to be a pop star.

00:01:28.968 --> 00:01:31.267
So -- I know, I know.

00:01:31.267 --> 00:01:34.400
And so I auditioned
for "American Idol."

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:35.534
-You did?

00:01:35.534 --> 00:01:36.767
-And the first time,
I didn't tell my mom

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:38.100
because I did
the online version,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.400
you know, when you could
submit your little tapes.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.534
I went in my sister's room

00:01:40.534 --> 00:01:42.501
and I put on my nice
little outfit, you know,

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:44.701
and I sang a little song,
and they didn't see it.

00:01:44.701 --> 00:01:47.100
And I was like, "It's because
of the fact it's on the Internet

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.300
and I need to go
to them in person."

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.334
So, the next time,
I went in the line

00:01:51.334 --> 00:01:52.434
and I waited for a full day,
did the whole thing,

00:01:52.434 --> 00:01:53.501
and my mom went with me.

00:01:53.501 --> 00:01:54.634
-Wait. I'm sorry.

00:01:54.634 --> 00:01:56.901
You auditioned
for "American Idol" twice?

00:01:56.901 --> 00:01:58.300
-Twice. And then I did
"The X Factor"

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.067
because they wasn't seeing
the vision.

00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:02.067
-Wait, wait, wait.
Wait. So you went to --

00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:03.734
[ Laughter ]

00:02:03.734 --> 00:02:05.901
So then you went
to "X Factor," as well?

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:07.167
-Yeah, because "American Idol,"
I was like,

00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:09.868
"Y'all don't understand
what's happening here.

00:02:09.868 --> 00:02:10.968
'X Factor' is going to get it."

00:02:10.968 --> 00:02:12.300
-Yeah. Did "X Factor" get it?

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:13.501
-Well, they -- No.

00:02:13.501 --> 00:02:15.767
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.767 --> 00:02:18.767
Can you believe it?

00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:20.467
-Can we talk about "Chevalier"?

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:22.801
-Yeah.
-You're getting rave reviews.

00:02:22.801 --> 00:02:24.367
If you want to plug your ears
for a second,

00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:25.701
I'll just read
a couple of these.

00:02:25.701 --> 00:02:27.834
Deadline says,
"Kelvin Harrison Jr.

00:02:27.834 --> 00:02:29.000
is a revelation."

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.834
Screen Rant says,
"Kelvin Harrison Jr.

00:02:30.834 --> 00:02:32.434
is magnetic,
a marvel in a role that

00:02:32.434 --> 00:02:34.467
solidifies him as
a leading man."

00:02:34.467 --> 00:02:35.767
Vogue put your movie on its

00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:38.901
"10 Biggest Films to Look
For in 2023" list.

00:02:38.901 --> 00:02:42.367
And The Root said that you might
be the next Denzel Washington.

00:02:42.367 --> 00:02:44.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:44.334 --> 00:02:45.701
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:47.968
-What?!

00:02:47.968 --> 00:02:51.667
How does that feel
when you hear that review?

00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:53.968
Do you go like, "Wow, Mom,

00:02:53.968 --> 00:02:55.534
I might be the next
Denzel Washington"?

00:02:55.534 --> 00:02:57.767
-I feel a little giddy,
a little giggly.

00:02:57.767 --> 00:02:59.334
-Yeah.

00:02:59.334 --> 00:03:02.334
-You know,
it's kind of -- It's surreal,

00:03:02.334 --> 00:03:03.968
I guess, in some ways,
but, at the same time,

00:03:03.968 --> 00:03:05.801
like, I take it as a compliment
in the sense

00:03:05.801 --> 00:03:08.200
that I guess it means
I'm choosing the right roles.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.801
You know, Denzel has always
chosen roles

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:13.434
that where men feel like
they have purpose

00:03:13.434 --> 00:03:16.000
and they feel dignified
and they feel dynamic.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.767
You know, I believe that,
as black people specifically,

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:20.067
we're not saints, nor sinners.

00:03:20.067 --> 00:03:21.267
We're all of the above,

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:23.467
and our humanity
should feel full.

00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:24.834
And those are
the types of projects

00:03:24.834 --> 00:03:26.467
I choose and the characters
I like to play,

00:03:26.467 --> 00:03:29.734
and if they think that makes me
Denzel-like, then I'll take it.

00:03:29.734 --> 00:03:30.834
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:30.834 --> 00:03:33.467
-And I know you said
you're a musician,

00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:35.501
but do you know
how to play the violin?

00:03:35.501 --> 00:03:36.567
Did you know...

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:38.434
-So, violin was
my first instrument.

00:03:38.434 --> 00:03:40.801
You saw, you know,
bald-headed me at 7.

00:03:40.801 --> 00:03:41.968
That was when I started.

00:03:41.968 --> 00:03:43.434
And then Hurricane Katrina
happened,

00:03:43.434 --> 00:03:45.000
so the program
that I was in went away.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.801
So I never got back into it,
because I was like,

00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:48.100
"It's not that cool
to play violin."

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.634
You know what I'm saying?

00:03:49.634 --> 00:03:53.567
But -- Because I didn't have no
Joseph Bologne to look towards.

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:55.801
But I basically
had to start up again.

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:57.234
I called my dad
and I was like,

00:03:57.234 --> 00:03:59.033
"Yo, they're trying to get me
to, you know, play this violin,"

00:03:59.033 --> 00:04:00.400
because I asked
Stephen Williams,

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.801
our director -- I was like,
"Can we get a double?"

00:04:03.801 --> 00:04:06.167
And he was like,
"No, we'll get a new actor."

00:04:06.167 --> 00:04:08.367
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:09.968
I said, "Well --"
-That's a good answer.

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:11.868
"Yeah, we'll get a double.
Yeah, we'll get a new actor.

00:04:11.868 --> 00:04:13.067
Bye."
-Right, right.

00:04:13.067 --> 00:04:14.801
So I said, "So, let's get
a plan together, Dad."

00:04:14.801 --> 00:04:17.100
And, you know,
we did five months,

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.667
seven days a week, six hours
a day of just violin training.

00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:23.167
And I only had to play
the songs in the movie,

00:04:23.167 --> 00:04:25.801
so, you know, it seems more
impressive than it actually is.

00:04:25.801 --> 00:04:28.634
-No, no, that's what a movie is.
You crushed it, buddy.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.200
-Thank you.
-You really did do a great job.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.601
-I appreciate it.
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:04:32.601 --> 00:04:35.067
Here's Kelvin Harrison Jr.
in "Chevalier."

00:04:35.067 --> 00:04:36.334
Take a look.

00:04:36.334 --> 00:04:38.701
♪♪

00:05:03.901 --> 00:05:07.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.667
I mean, Dad,
you watching that, Dad?

00:05:11.968 --> 00:05:14.267
Hey, congratulations.
Thanks for coming on.

00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:15.934
Kelvin Harrison Jr., everybody.

00:05:15.934 --> 00:05:18.834
"Chevalier"
hits theaters Friday.

00:05:18.834 --> 00:05:20.300
We'll be right back with
more "Tonight Show."

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.968
Stick around. Well done.

